>rjmr@st-andrews.ac.uk</email></corresp><fn fn-type="edited-by"><p>Edited by Stephen C. Harrison, Children's Hospital, Boston, MA, and approved October 1, 2008</p></fn><fn fn-type="con"><p>Author contributions: R.J.R., S.J.G., and J.J.S. designed research; R.J.R., P.S.K., and D.J.S. performed research; R.J.R., D.A.S., S.R.M., S.J.G., and J.J.S. analyzed data; and R.J.R., D.A.S., S.J.G., and J.J.S. wrote the paper.</p></fn><fn fn-type="equal" id="FN1"><p><sup>1</sup>R.J.R. and P.S.K. contributed equally to this work.</p></fn><fn fn-type="conflict"><p>The authors declare no conflict of interest.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>18</day><month>11</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>12</day><month>11</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><volume>105</volume><issue>46</issue><issue-id pub-id-type="pmc-issue-id">173657</issue-id><fpage>17736</fpage><lpage>17741</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>23</day><month>7</month><year>2008</year></date></history><pub-history><event event-type="pmc-release"><date><day>18</day><month>05</month><year>2009</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-live"><date><day>18</day><month>05</month><year>2009</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-last-change"><date iso-8601-date="2026-09-21 00:06:55.473"><day>21</day><month>09</month><year>2026</year></date></event></pub-history><permissions><copyright-statement>© 2008 by The National Academy of Sciences of the USA</copyright-statement></permissions><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="pmc-pdf" xlink:href="zpq17736.pdf"/><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:title="pdf" xlink:href="zpq04608017736.pdf"/><related-article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" related-article-type="commentary" xml:lang="en" xlink:title="article-commentary" vol="105" page="18647" journal-id="Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A" journal-id-type="nlm-ta" ext-link-type="pmc" xlink:href="PMC2596271"><article-title>How influenza virus is locked out of the cell</article-title><volume>105</volume><issue>48</issue><date><day>24</day><month>11</month><year>2008</year></date><fpage>18647</fpage><lpage>18648</lpage><source>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America</source><pub-id pub-id-type="pmcid">PMC2596271</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19033206</pub-id></related-article><abstract><p>The influenza surface glycoprotein hemagglutinin (HA) is a potential target for antiviral drugs because of its key roles in the initial stages of infection: receptor binding and the fusion of virus and cell membranes. The structure of HA in complex with a known inhibitor of membrane fusion and virus infectivity, <italic toggle="yes">tert</italic>-butyl hydroquinone (TBHQ), shows that the inhibitor binds in a hydrophobic pocket formed at an interface between HA monomers. Occupation of this site by TBHQ stabilizes the neutral pH structure through intersubunit and intrasubunit interactions that presumably inhibit the conformational rearrangements required for membrane fusion. The nature of the binding site suggests routes for the chemical modification of TBHQ that could lead to the development of more potent inhibitors of membrane fusion and potential anti-influenza drugs.</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>crystallography</kwd><kwd>drug design</kwd></kwd-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-qastatus</meta-name><meta-value>0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-live</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-embargo</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-released</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-open-access</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-olf</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>p